course: biology
lesson: 342
title: Where eggs come from: hens, shell colours, sizes and what is inside
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A box of six eggs is almost a week of work for one hen. A modern laying hen can lay about 300 eggs a year, nearly one a day, while her wild ancestor, the red junglefowl of Asia, lays only a few clutches of five or six.
explain: Each egg takes about a day to make. The yolk travels down a long tube inside the hen, gets wrapped in the white and thin skins, and spends about 20 hours in the last part getting its hard shell of calcium, the same mineral as in chalk and in your bones.
explain: Crack an egg into a glass bowl and you can count its parts: the yellow yolk, the clear white, and two twisty white cords that hold the yolk in the middle like the ropes of a hammock.
explain: Shop eggs never hatch, because most come from hens kept without a cockerel, so there is no chick inside. And the shell colour, white, brown or even blue, only tells you the breed of hen, not the taste.
fact: A modern laying hen can lay about 300 eggs a year, nearly one a day, because farmers kept the best layers for hundreds of years; her wild ancestor, the red junglefowl, lays only a few clutches of about five or six.
fact: Shell colour depends on the breed of hen; inside, a brown egg and a white egg are the same food, and as a rough rule hens with white earlobes lay white eggs and hens with red earlobes lay brown ones.
fact: Breeds such as the Araucana and the Cream Legbar lay blue or greenish eggs, and in a blue egg the colour goes right through the shell, while brown is mostly on the outside layers.
fact: A hen lays eggs whether or not there is a cockerel, and most shop eggs were never fertilised, so they have no chick inside.
fact: Even a fertilised egg must be kept at about 37 to 38 degrees for 21 days to hatch.
fact: An egg has a yellow yolk, the food store for a chick, a clear white that is mostly water and protein and cushions it, and two twisty cords called chalazae that hold the yolk in the middle.
fact: A shell has thousands of tiny pores; over weeks water escapes, the air pocket at the blunt end grows, and a very old egg floats in water while a fresh one lies flat on the bottom.
fact: A chick takes its first breath from the air pocket before it breaks the shell.
fact: Double-yolk eggs come most often from young hens whose egg-making has not settled, so two yolks are released at once.
fact: In Britain eggs are sorted by weight into small, medium, large and very large, a large egg weighing roughly 63 to 73 grams, and older hens tend to lay bigger eggs.
fact: An ostrich egg holds as much as about two dozen hen's eggs, and a hummingbird egg is about the size of a jellybean.
fact: The code stamped on a British egg starts with how the hen lived: 0 organic, 1 free range, 2 barn, 3 cage; then letters for the country and a number for the farm, and a red lion stamp means the hens were vaccinated against Salmonella, a germ that gives people a bad tummy upset.
fact: An egg takes about a day to make, with about 20 hours spent getting its shell, which is mostly calcium, so hens need lots of calcium and backyard hens are given crushed oyster shell.
exercise: About how many eggs can a modern laying hen lay in a year? | About 300 | About 30 | About 6 | About 3000
exercise: Which wild bird is the ancestor of the farm hen? | The red junglefowl of Asia | The pheasant of Europe | The ostrich of Africa | The wild turkey of America
exercise: What does the colour of an eggshell depend on? | The breed of the hen | What the hen ate that morning | How old the egg is | Whether a chick is inside
exercise: Which hen breed lays blue or greenish eggs? | The Araucana | The Silkie | The red junglefowl | Every old hen
exercise: Why do shop eggs not hatch into chicks? | Most were never fertilised, as the hens had no cockerel | The cold of the fridge slowly turns the little chick back into yolk | The shop shakes the chicks out | The shell is too hard for a chick
exercise: How long must a fertilised hen's egg be kept warm to hatch? | About 21 days | About 2 days | About 6 months | About one hour
exercise: What are the two twisty white cords in an egg for? | They hold the yolk in the middle of the egg | They are the chick's first legs, already starting to grow | They feed air to the yolk | They are bits of broken shell
exercise: What is the yolk for? | It is the food store for a chick | It is the soft start of the chick's shell | It is the egg's air pocket | It is just colour to make cooking look nice
exercise: Why does a very old egg float in water? | Water has escaped through the pores and the air pocket has grown | The yolk has slowly turned into air and gas inside the shell | Old eggs fill up with water through the shell and get lighter | The shell gets thicker with age
exercise: Where is the air pocket of an egg? | At the blunt end | At the pointed end | Inside the yolk | In the middle of the white
exercise: Which hens most often lay double-yolk eggs? | Young hens whose egg-making has not settled | Very old hens that are coming near the end of their laying | Hens that eat too much corn | Hens kept with two cockerels
exercise: How are eggs sorted into small, medium, large and very large in Britain? | By weight | By the colour of the shell | By the age of the hen | By the length of the egg
exercise: What does a number 1 at the start of a British egg code mean? | The hen was free range | The hen was kept in a cage | The egg was laid on a Monday | It is the farm's first egg
exercise: What does the red lion stamp on British eggs show? | The hens were vaccinated against Salmonella | The egg came from a lion's enclosure at the zoo | The egg is extra large | The hen won a prize as a champion layer
exercise: What is an eggshell mostly made of? | Calcium | Plastic-like protein | Sugar | Iron
exercise: Why are backyard hens given crushed oyster shell? | It gives them calcium for strong eggshells | It scrubs and cleans their beaks when they peck | It makes their yolks a deep orange | It stops them laying too much
test: In a cartoon, a hen lays an egg and a fluffy chick pops out a second later. What is wrong? | A fertilised egg needs about 21 days of warmth before it hatches | Nothing is wrong, because chicks always hatch at once as soon as the egg is laid | Chicks hatch only from brown eggs | Chicks hatch after 21 hours in the fridge
test: «brown eggs are healthier innit, that's why mum buys em» What is the best answer? | No, the colour only shows the breed; inside they are the same food | Yes, brown eggs have more vitamins and goodness packed inside them | Yes, white eggs are bleached at the farm | No, white eggs are healthier
test: A hen has red earlobes. As a rough rule, what colour eggs will she lay? | Brown | White | Blue | Spotted
test: He cracks a brown egg and a blue egg and looks at the inside of the shells. What does he see? | The brown shell is often white inside, the blue one is blue inside too | Both are white inside | Both are brown inside | The blue one is white inside and the brown one brown
test: «crap, dunno how old these eggs are» How can a bowl of water help? | Fresh eggs lie flat on the bottom, very old ones float | Old eggs sink faster to the bottom than fresh ones | Fresh eggs float on top and the very old ones sink right down | Old eggs turn the water yellow
test: Count them: he cracks 3 eggs, and one has a double yolk. How many yolks are in the bowl? | 4 | 3 | 6 | 5
test: «no rooster in our coop so the hens won't lay» Is that right? | No, hens lay eggs without a cockerel; the eggs just cannot hatch | Yes, no cockerel means no eggs at all, not even one in the whole year | Yes, but they lay once a year | No, the hens lay only brown eggs then
test: How many hen's eggs would you need to make an omelette as big as one ostrich egg? | About 24 | About 2 | About 6 | About 200
test: What would happen to a hen's eggshells if she got too little calcium in her food? | They would be thin and weak or even soft | They would slowly turn a pale blue colour | They would get thicker and much harder | They would have no yolks
test: Count them: 4 hens peck in the yard, each on two legs. How many legs and how many wings? | 8 legs and 8 wings | 4 legs and 8 wings | 8 legs and 4 wings | 16 legs and 8 wings
test: «why's the wild one only lay a few eggs, lazy?» Why does the red junglefowl lay far fewer eggs than a farm hen? | Farmers bred hens for centuries, always keeping the best layers | Wild birds are too lazy to lay and would rather spend the day looking for food | Wild birds lay eggs only once in their lives | Junglefowl eggs are ten times bigger
test: A chick is ready to hatch but has not cracked the shell yet. Where does it take its first breath? | From the air pocket at the blunt end | Through a little hole it bites in the side of the shell | From the yolk | It does not breathe until it is out
exam: Identify the principal reason for which eggs offered for retail sale do not ordinarily hatch. | The majority are laid by hens maintained without a cockerel and are therefore unfertilised | The process of refrigeration converts the embryo into yolk | The shell is rendered impenetrable during packaging | Retailers remove the embryo prior to sale
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the colour of the eggshell? | It is determined by the breed of the hen and does not indicate the nutritional value of the contents | It is determined by the freshness of the egg, darker shells indicating that the egg was laid more recently | It indicates that the egg is fertilised | It indicates the quantity of calcium consumed on the day of laying
exam: Ascertain the reason for which an aged egg floats when placed in water. | Water has escaped through the pores of the shell and the air space at the blunt end has enlarged | The yolk has been converted into gas | The shell has absorbed water and become lighter | The white has hardened into a buoyant solid
exam: State the significance of the numeral 0 appearing first in the code stamped upon a British egg. | The egg was produced by a hen kept under organic conditions | The egg was produced by a hen confined in a cage | The egg was laid on the first day of the week | The egg contains no yolk
